# Build & CI Parity Rules

This project ships two build systems (`Makefile` and `CMakeLists.txt`) and claims multiple platform/toolchain targets. Drift between them is the biggest real risk to "cross-platform" claims actually being true.

## Every source/header change touches both build systems, in the same change

Adding, removing, or renaming a file under `src/` or `include/carmen/` requires updating, in the same commit:

- `Makefile`: `LIB_SRCS`, and `PUBLIC_HEADERS` if it's a public header
- `CMakeLists.txt`: `CARMEN_SOURCES`, and `CARMEN_PUBLIC_HEADERS` if it's a public header
- Both `TEST_BINS`/`COV_BINS` (Makefile) and a matching `carmen_add_test(...)` call (CMakeLists.txt) if it's a new test module

A PR that updates only one build system is incomplete, even if it happens to build locally.

## A platform claimed as "supported" must have a CI job that builds and tests it

`README.md` currently claims GCC 8+, Clang 7+, and MSVC 2019+. If a platform/compiler isn't built and tested in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, treat the README claim as aspirational and either add the CI job or fix the claim -- don't leave them silently out of sync.

## New sanitizer/flag support belongs in both build systems

If you add a flag or target to one build system (e.g. a `CARMEN_SANITIZE` CMake option, or a `test-sanitize` Makefile target for ASan/UBSan), add the equivalent capability to the other build system too, even if the mechanism necessarily differs between Make and CMake.

## CI should exercise the sanitized build, not just plain `test`/`coverage`

`make test` and `make coverage`/CTest tell you what ran; they don't tell you if what ran hit undefined behavior. A CI job running `make test-sanitize` (or the CMake `CARMEN_SANITIZE` equivalent) is required alongside the plain test job, not a substitute for it.

## Version & SONAME parity

The top-level `VERSION` file is the single source of truth (see `doc/versioning.md`). Both build systems must read it and derive their shared-library versioning from it:

- The recorded SONAME/`install_name` must be the `.$(SOMAJOR)` form, identical across Make (`SHARED_FLAGS`) and CMake (`VERSION`/`SOVERSION` set on target `carmen`): `libcarmen.so.$(SOMAJOR)` and `@rpath/libcarmen.$(SOMAJOR).dylib`.
- Both build systems must lay out the same three artifacts — the versioned real file (`libcarmen.X.Y.Z.so` / `libcarmen.X.Y.Z.dylib`), the soname symlink, and the dev symlink — in `dist/` and in the installation prefix.
- `carmen_version.h` is generated (never committed) from `VERSION` by `tools/gen_version.sh`; a change to versioning in one build system requires the matching change in the other.

Guard it in CI: `make version-check` (runs inside `make test`) and CTest `version_check` flag any disagreement between `VERSION`, the generated header, and the resolved version; `make verify-soname` (otool/readelf) enforces the recorded-name parity on both Linux and macOS.
